1
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:05,230
Hello, everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:06,480
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:09,430
My name is Katherine and today we have a very special lesson for you.

4
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:13,850
This is actually the first in a series of lessons about interview skills.

5
00:00:14,270 --> 00:00:14,770
That's right.

6
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:34,870
For 10 lessons that you will hear once a week for the next 10 weeks, we're going to take a look at an interview, a job interview and we're going to take you step by step throughout the whole interview process as well as teaching you some useful words and phrases that you can use next time you're at a job interview in English.

7
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:35,530
Exactly.

8
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:37,440
So pay attention.

9
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:49,030
Today is the first day we're going to be entering the office and we're going to learn some things that are important for talking to your future employer or meeting people in an interview.

10
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:57,760
Come in.

11
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:00,800
Mr. Parsons?

12
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:01,800
Ah, you must be Rebecca.

13
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:02,880
Please do come in.

14
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:06,760
Thank you for making some time to see me, Mr. Parsons.

15
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:08,660
It's a pleasure to meet you finally.

16
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:10,580
The pleasure is all mine, Rebecca.

17
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:11,640
Have a seat, please.

18
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:15,920
Now would you like any refreshments, tea or coffee?

19
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:17,340
A coffee would be lovely.

20
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:18,020
Thank you.

21
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:19,100
Black, no sugar.

22
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:20,220
No problem.

23
00:01:22,930 --> 00:01:24,790
Sally, can we have two coffees, please?

24
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:26,610
One, no milk or sugar.

25
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:29,400
Certainly, Mr. Parsons.

26
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:33,870
So Rebecca, I understand you had a first interview with Ms. Childs last week?

27
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:34,950
Yes, that's correct.

28
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,710
She filled me in on the details of the job on the telephone.

29
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:38,650
Great.

30
00:01:39,170 --> 00:01:43,210
Well, I'm glad to say she recommended you for a second interview and here we are.

31
00:01:43,810 --> 00:01:47,970
Perhaps we can start by discussing your background and resume details a little.

32
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:48,990
Yes, of course.

33
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:53,810
Alright, we're back.

34
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:56,570
So now let's take a look at language takeaway.

35
00:01:59,370 --> 00:02:00,490
Language takeaway.

36
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:09,140
So one of the first things that you're offered when you go to interview at a company is refreshments.

37
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:10,020
Right.

38
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:11,600
Would you like any refreshments?

39
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:17,940
This sounds complicated, but it's actually a very simple word for beverages or drinks.

40
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:18,600
Right.

41
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:21,640
So that's why he offered her tea or coffee.

42
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:22,420
Right.

43
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:25,680
So refreshments are cola, coffee, tea.

44
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:27,260
But how do you answer this?

45
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:30,960
If I say, would you like any refreshments, Marco?

46
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:33,090
You can say, yes, water would be fine.

47
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:33,590
Thank you.

48
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:35,440
Okay.

49
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:36,440
Water would be fine.

50
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:36,570
Or I'd like a Coke.

51
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:39,110
Or I'd like a cup of coffee.

52
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:40,070
Cup of coffee.

53
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:41,720
Great.

54
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:42,720
So that's how you answer drinks.

55
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:47,830
And after that, the characters are talking about the details of the job.

56
00:02:48,310 --> 00:02:50,390
So what are these details?

57
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:52,050
How are they different from the job itself?

58
00:02:52,670 --> 00:02:57,330
Well, the details of the job are the specific points about the job.

59
00:02:57,570 --> 00:03:08,350
So for example, if she applied for a job as a receptionist, the details of the job would be answering the phones, taking messages, receiving people at the office.

60
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,680
Those are the main details of the job.

61
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:19,520
Also, we need someone who works from 9am to 5pm, someone who is going to come five days a week.

62
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:23,980
So the details are basically more information about something.

63
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:25,160
Right.

64
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:27,200
The specific information about a general topic.

65
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:29,040
And it's not only related to work.

66
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,480
The details can be of many, many things.

67
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:32,140
Right.

68
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:37,480
So if I ask you about your details, Marco, you could maybe tell me your phone number, your address.

69
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:38,840
My full name.

70
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,000
Your full name or birthday.

71
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:42,240
So these are details.

72
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:43,240
Okay.

73
00:03:43,240 --> 00:04:00,260
And well, after they talked about the details of the job, after she explained that she received the details of the job by phone, Mr. Parson said that she was recommended for a second interview.

74
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:04,980
So Rebecca is coming to the office for a second time.

75
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:07,020
She already had one interview.

76
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:12,720
And the woman she talked to the first time recommended her for a second interview.

77
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:15,200
So the verb here is to recommend.

78
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:15,900
Right.

79
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:20,220
And you can recommend a person, an object, a movie.

80
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:28,260
And that's basically you are saying that this is good and that it's worth seeing, it's worth buying, it's worth interviewing.

81
00:04:28,890 --> 00:04:31,230
For example, I really like the movie Avatar.

82
00:04:32,050 --> 00:04:33,810
Marco, I recommend you see it.

83
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:34,330
Okay.

84
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:39,400
And you can say, Catherine recommended the movie Avatar.

85
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:39,650
Okay.

86
00:04:40,050 --> 00:04:41,730
So Catherine liked it.

87
00:04:41,970 --> 00:04:46,310
Catherine decided, Catherine suggested or told Marco it would be good.

88
00:04:46,530 --> 00:04:46,970
That's right.

89
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:48,550
So to recommend.

90
00:04:49,670 --> 00:04:54,170
And then Mr. Parson said that they're going to start discussing her background.

91
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:57,240
Well, background, think of the word back.

92
00:04:57,560 --> 00:04:58,840
That means behind.

93
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:07,900
This usually means a person's history at a job or at many jobs or even their educational background, information about where they studied.

94
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:08,800
That's right.

95
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:21,000
So the background, maybe a person's background is, as you said, all their history, work, study, many, many different things, even maybe seminars or courses that they assisted.

96
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:23,280
Or even just their personal history.

97
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,030
For example, Marco, you speak English like an American.

98
00:05:26,570 --> 00:05:28,650
What's your background because I know you're not American.

99
00:05:28,650 --> 00:05:29,030
Right.

100
00:05:29,250 --> 00:05:29,650
I'm from Ecuador.

101
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:31,840
Okay.

102
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:32,840
Background.

103
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:33,840
So that means someone's personal history.

104
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:34,840
That's right.

105
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:41,390
And hand in hand with background, we have a piece of paper that usually you send into an employer to apply for a job.

106
00:05:41,870 --> 00:05:43,350
This is the famous resume.

107
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:44,490
Resume.

108
00:05:44,810 --> 00:05:45,870
So you're right, Marco.

109
00:05:46,110 --> 00:05:47,170
Resume is a document.

110
00:05:47,750 --> 00:05:53,390
In this case, it is a document that lists your work history, your working background.

111
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:56,610
And it's a very important part of applying for a job.

112
00:05:56,920 --> 00:06:09,400
And usually you send your resume before you go for the interview so that the employer can look and see is this person qualified, how old are they, what's their work history.

113
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:12,770
And well, very similar to a resume, you may have heard a CV.

114
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:14,200
That's right.

115
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:16,420
A CV is a lot like a resume.

116
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:21,280
It stands for curriculum C, vitae V, CV.

117
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:23,700
And it's more detailed.

118
00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:28,400
It has more details about a person's academic or study history.

119
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:29,400
Exactly.

120
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:35,360
So a resume, I guess it's very similar to a summary, a summary of your work background.

121
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:44,400
Whereas a CV will have more details about your education, maybe more details about your work background, what did you do, objectives, etc.

122
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:45,400
Great.

123
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:47,450
So maybe we should listen to today's dialogue one more time.

124
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:52,570
And when we come back, we'll be talking about some of the interesting phrases we heard in today's dialogue.

125
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:01,560
Come in.

126
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:03,990
Mr. Parsons.

127
00:07:04,590 --> 00:07:05,590
Ah, you must be Rebecca.

128
00:07:06,170 --> 00:07:06,730
Please do come in.

129
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:11,240
Thank you for making some time to see me, Mr. Parsons.

130
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:12,520
It's a pleasure to meet you finally.

131
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:14,360
The pleasure is all mine, Rebecca.

132
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:15,500
Have a seat, please.

133
00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:19,790
Now would you like any refreshments, tea or coffee?

134
00:07:20,210 --> 00:07:21,870
A coffee would be lovely, thank you.

135
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:22,950
Black, no sugar.

136
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:24,090
No problem.

137
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:28,580
Sally, can we have two coffees, please?

138
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:30,440
One, no milk or sugar.

139
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:33,240
Certainly, Mr. Parsons.

140
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:37,700
So Rebecca, I understand you had a first interview with Ms. Childs last week?

141
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:38,840
Yes, that's correct.

142
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:41,520
She filled me in on the details of the job on the telephone.

143
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:42,520
Great.

144
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:46,000
Well, I'm glad to say she recommended you for a second interview.

145
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:47,020
And here we are.

146
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,860
Perhaps we can start by discussing your background and resume details a little.

147
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:52,840
Yes, of course.

148
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:59,240
Alright, we're back.

149
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:03,460
So now we have three phrases that we're going to take a look at on Fluency Builder.

150
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:06,040
Fluency Builder.

151
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:13,440
Alright, so when Rebecca arrived, Mr. Parsons said, ah, you must be Rebecca.

152
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:14,680
Please do come in.

153
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:17,140
Okay, so you could say, please come in.

154
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:21,520
This means, please come into my office or come on in.

155
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:21,900
Right.

156
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:24,840
But here, the interesting thing about this is the word do.

157
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:27,920
Right, he's using the verb do and it may sound wrong.

158
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:33,300
It may sound like it's unnecessary because you do have a phrasal verb there already, come in.

159
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:35,280
So you can just say, please come in.

160
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:41,530
But when he says, please do come in, he is emphasizing on her to come in.

161
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:42,230
That's right.

162
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:45,610
So this is a very polite way to invite someone inside.

163
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:48,650
You can also say, please do have a seat.

164
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:55,120
Alright, so again, you are emphasizing in a very polite way to please sit down.

165
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:56,120
That's right.

166
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:56,450
Please do come in.

167
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:58,650
So there are many other ways that you can use it.

168
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:06,410
So for example, if you really want your friend to call you sometime next time you're in town, say please do call me next time you're in town.

169
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:07,230
Please do.

170
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:11,170
You could even say it afterwards, simply please do for emphasis.

171
00:09:11,710 --> 00:09:11,910
Right.

172
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:15,960
So if I say, hey, Catherine, I'm going to call you next time I'm in Shanghai.

173
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:16,110
Please do.

174
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:17,960
There you go.

175
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:18,310
So that means, please do call.

176
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:21,160
Alright, so please do come in.

177
00:09:21,250 --> 00:09:21,950
Please do call.

178
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:23,560
Alright.

179
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:28,640
After that, Rebecca says, thank you for making some time to see me, Mr. Parsons.

180
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:33,160
So she is happy that Mr. Parsons is available and wants to talk to her.

181
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:38,420
This is a very common phrase that means, thank you so much for interviewing me.

182
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:38,860
Right.

183
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:44,940
So I guess it's kind of a way of saying, look, I know that you're a busy person and that you don't have very much time.

184
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:47,760
So thank you for making some time to see me.

185
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:48,960
Right.

186
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:49,780
So this isn't just about interviews.

187
00:09:50,120 --> 00:09:56,080
You could say this phrase for meeting with a client or meeting with even a friend.

188
00:09:56,680 --> 00:10:01,660
You could say, hey, Marco, I know you were really busy last week, but thank you so much for making some time to see me.

189
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:03,120
Right.

190
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:09,080
So it's just basically saying, I understand that you're very busy, but I appreciate that you made some time to see me.

191
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:10,520
Mm hmm.

192
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:11,520
All right.

193
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:12,560
And well, she said, you know what?

194
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:17,880
It's a pleasure to meet you, but Mr. Parsons replied by saying, the pleasure is all mine.

195
00:10:18,280 --> 00:10:18,560
All right.

196
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:21,340
Another very, very polite phrase that you hear a lot.

197
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:27,900
Maybe some of you have heard the phrase, it's a pleasure to meet you before, but the response, the answer is very interesting.

198
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:29,860
The pleasure is all mine.

199
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:30,520
Right.

200
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:33,840
So this stands for the pleasure is all mine.

201
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:34,840
Mm hmm.

202
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:35,840
Right.

203
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:45,960
And I guess it's the most appropriate response if somebody says, it's a pleasure to meet you, because if you don't say this, I think you may sound a little bit arrogant.

204
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:48,200
Well, you could say it's a pleasure to meet you too.

205
00:10:48,540 --> 00:10:48,660
Right.

206
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:54,920
But this is another way to answer the person because you don't always want to say it's a pleasure to meet you.

207
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:55,880
It's a pleasure to meet you too.

208
00:10:56,260 --> 00:10:56,380
Right.

209
00:10:56,700 --> 00:11:00,870
This is a way to say something different and to say, well, the pleasure is all mine.

210
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:02,040
Right.

211
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:03,010
But it's very important to answer something, right?

212
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:06,310
Because if I say, it's a pleasure to meet you and you say, thank you.

213
00:11:06,550 --> 00:11:08,830
It's like, no, that is a little bit arrogant.

214
00:11:09,270 --> 00:11:10,350
You have to say something.

215
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:11,560
Right.

216
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:14,520
And we recommend that you say this or you just say the same thing back.

217
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:15,370
So it's nice to meet you.

218
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:16,850
Oh, it's nice to meet you too.

219
00:11:17,050 --> 00:11:17,230
Right.

220
00:11:17,890 --> 00:11:18,270
Okay.

221
00:11:18,530 --> 00:11:24,050
And our last phrase is actually when they were talking about, again, the details of the job.

222
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:28,410
And she said that Miss Childs filled her in on the details.

223
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:32,690
She said, she filled me in on the details of the job on the telephone.

224
00:11:33,510 --> 00:11:33,950
Okay.

225
00:11:33,990 --> 00:11:40,930
So this is an interesting verb, to fill in, or in this case, to fill someone in, to fill me in, to fill you in.

226
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:44,290
This basically means to tell someone about something.

227
00:11:44,570 --> 00:11:51,990
Or if I know a little bit of information, but not a lot of information about something, this way you're telling me more so I understand it better.

228
00:11:52,150 --> 00:11:52,270
Right.

229
00:11:52,390 --> 00:11:53,590
I'm giving you all the information.

230
00:11:54,160 --> 00:12:03,160
So maybe there is somebody is talking about a rumor in the office and you go and you say, hey, fill me in on the gossip.

231
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:03,820
All right.

232
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:05,340
So you want to know all about it.

233
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:06,600
Right.

234
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:11,600
So you go, we're having a party next Sunday, but I'll have to fill you in on the details around Friday.

235
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:11,940
Okay.

236
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:13,800
Because you don't have all the information right now.

237
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:14,800
Yeah.

238
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:15,440
I don't know yet what time or where.

239
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:16,160
Okay.

240
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,280
So to fill someone in is to give them all the information or to give them more information.

241
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:22,430
Fill me in.

242
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:23,230
Fill me in.

243
00:12:24,040 --> 00:12:25,040
All right.

244
00:12:25,040 --> 00:12:28,030
Why don't we take a look at our dialogue for the last time and we'll be back in a little bit.

245
00:12:36,710 --> 00:12:37,250
Come in.

246
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:39,640
Mr. Parsons.

247
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:41,260
Ah, you must be Rebecca.

248
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:42,400
Please do come in.

249
00:12:43,910 --> 00:12:46,310
Thank you for making some time to see me, Mr. Parsons.

250
00:12:46,770 --> 00:12:47,790
It's a pleasure to meet you.

251
00:12:47,930 --> 00:12:49,850
Finally, the pleasure is all mine.

252
00:12:49,970 --> 00:12:51,170
Rebecca, have a seat, please.

253
00:12:52,830 --> 00:12:55,430
Now would you like any refreshments, tea or coffee?

254
00:12:55,970 --> 00:12:56,870
A coffee would be lovely.

255
00:12:57,210 --> 00:12:57,530
Thank you.

256
00:12:57,830 --> 00:12:58,610
Black, no sugar.

257
00:12:59,170 --> 00:12:59,730
No problem.

258
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:04,360
Sally, can we have two coffees, please?

259
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:06,140
One, no milk or sugar.

260
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:08,920
Certainly, Mr. Parsons.

261
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:13,400
So Rebecca, I understand you had a first interview with Ms. Childs last week?

262
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:14,480
Yes, that's correct.

263
00:13:15,020 --> 00:13:17,220
She filled me in on the details of the job on the telephone.

264
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:18,180
Great.

265
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:22,740
Well, I'm glad to say she recommended you for a second interview and here we are.

266
00:13:23,300 --> 00:13:27,500
Perhaps we can start by discussing your background and resume details a little.

267
00:13:27,820 --> 00:13:28,520
Yes, of course.

268
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:44,080
Alright, so as we mentioned, this is the first part of a ten-part series of the interview series and this is going to be very, very helpful.

269
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:53,350
As you saw in this first part, we're just taking a look at some basic vocab and phrases that you can use when you're just introducing yourself, you're just getting started in the interview.

270
00:13:54,520 --> 00:14:16,060
So, what we're going to be looking at later on is talking about yourself, answering questions, but many people don't realize that one of the most important parts of the interview is the very, very beginning because some psychologists say that we make a decision about a person or how we like a person within the first, I think, what, 30 seconds of talking to them?

271
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:17,480
I think so, yeah.

272
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:25,130
So, it's important that you speak confidently and that you speak, say some of these English phrases comfortably.

273
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:27,350
It's a pleasure to meet you.

274
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:29,200
Oh, it's the pleasure is mine.

275
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:30,790
These little things will really help you.

276
00:14:30,990 --> 00:14:31,450
That's right.

277
00:14:31,630 --> 00:14:44,430
And I think as talking about introductions and how this woman arrived to the office, I think one of the key things that we would recommend is to look that person in the eye and give a firm handshake.

278
00:14:44,750 --> 00:14:49,510
I don't know how many people actually realize how important this is.

279
00:14:49,750 --> 00:15:03,210
It's very, very important because as an interviewer, the person or the manager who's interviewing you, I think you can tell a lot from a person if somebody gives you a very flimsy handshake or doesn't look you in the eye.

280
00:15:03,690 --> 00:15:04,130
That's right.

281
00:15:04,350 --> 00:15:12,570
Well, I think in many ways people think, oh, this person is not confident or this person is uncomfortable or this person is hiding something.

282
00:15:13,310 --> 00:15:22,510
And so, it's important really to be yourself, to smile, to look at a person in the eyes, especially in a lot of like American companies or Canadian companies.

283
00:15:22,970 --> 00:15:30,710
I know in other parts of the world, smiling with a stranger is unusual or maybe unacceptable, but in America it means I'm confident.

284
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:32,160
Here I am.

285
00:15:32,160 --> 00:15:32,370
Here's my name.

286
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:33,390
Nice to meet you.

287
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:35,730
It's a very important part of an introduction.

288
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:37,190
Yes, very true.

289
00:15:37,530 --> 00:15:39,350
So we actually want to hear what you think.

290
00:15:39,850 --> 00:15:48,450
How important for you is a handshake, a firm handshake, looking someone in the eye or how do interviews work in your country?

291
00:15:48,710 --> 00:15:53,830
Let us know and we hope that this is helpful for you in the future if you're looking for jobs or you just want to practice your English.

292
00:15:54,030 --> 00:15:54,330
That's right.

293
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:58,560
So, if you have any other questions or doubts, we're at EnglishPod.com.

294
00:15:58,560 --> 00:15:59,230
We'll see everyone there.

295
00:15:59,560 --> 00:16:00,560
Bye.

296
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:08,150
Bye.
